{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing, the model underpredicts levels across most of the 30-day horizon. Apply an upward level shift (larger in the first ~20 days).",
    "Trend-lag: the forecast path reacts too slowly to regime changes (especially transitions into strong rallies). Increase forecast slope when recent momentum is positive; decrease slope when momentum turns negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: underprediction is strongest in the mid-horizon (~days 5–25). Use a hump-shaped uplift (small at day 1, larger mid-horizon, tapering toward day 30).",
    "Post-spike overshoot risk: after sharp upward moves, the model sometimes extrapolates too high at the far end (e.g., day 25–30) relative to realized pullbacks. Add mild mean-reversion damping to the last third of the horizon when recent volatility/spike magnitude is high.",
    "Volatility underestimation: realized outcomes show larger swings and higher peaks than forecasts during rallies. Widen forecast distribution / add convexity: uplift more on high-momentum days than on low-momentum days rather than a flat shift."
  ]
}
